<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charles Albert of Sardinia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1798">1798</a>–<a href="/years/1849">1849</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-savoy">House of Savoy</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Charles Albert was King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> from 1831. In 1848 he granted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/statuto-albertino">Statuto Albertino</a>, a constitution that later became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>'s, and declared war on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> in support of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> revolt. Defeated at Custoza in 1848 and Novara in 1849, he abdicated in favour of his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-emmanuel-ii">Victor Emmanuel II</a> and died in exile in Portugal.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-albert-of-sardinia.jpg" alt="Charles Albert of Sardinia" width="320" height="482" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ferdinando Cavalleri · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Carlo_Alberto_by_Cavalleri.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Charles Albert was born on 2 October 1798 at the Palazzo Carignano in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a>, the son of Charles Emmanuel, Prince of Carignano, and Maria Cristina of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>. The Carignano line was a junior branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-savoy">House of Savoy</a>, so his chances of succeeding to the kingdom were slim at his birth. His father, sympathetic to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberalism">liberalism</a>, joined the French cause after the invasion of 1796, and the family was sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and kept under surveillance. Charles Albert grew up there and received a liberal education.</p><p>In March 1821 young liberal officers met him as they planned to force a constitution on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-emmanuel-i-of-sardinia">Victor Emmanuel I</a>. When the king abdicated, Charles Albert was made regent, but he left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a> secretly on the night of 21 March, and Charles Felix ordered him to Tuscany. To show his penitence he joined the French expedition against the Spanish liberals in 1823.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Charles Albert became king on 27 April 1831, when Charles Felix died without an heir and the direct line of Savoy came to an end. Alarmed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/july-revolution">July Revolution</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, he made an alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, signed on 23 July 1831. His rule was conservative at first, yet he set up a Council of State, abolished torture and had new codes drawn up: a civil code in 1837, a criminal code in 1839, and commercial and procedural codes in 1842.</p><p>In 1848, as revolutions spread across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, he granted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/statuto-albertino">Albertine Statute</a>, which remained in force until 1947. On 23 March 1848 he issued a proclamation to the people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veneto">Veneto</a> and sent his troops against the Austrians in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-italian-war-of-independence">First Italian War of Independence</a>. Pope Pius IX and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-ii-of-the-two-sicilies">Ferdinand II of the Two Sicilies</a> abandoned the cause, and he was defeated at Novara in 1849.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After the defeat Charles Albert abdicated in favour of his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-emmanuel-ii">Victor Emmanuel II</a>. He left Novara just after midnight on 23 March 1849; stopped at an Austrian roadblock, he gave his name as the Count of Barge, a title he held, and was allowed to pass. He reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porto">Porto</a> in Portugal, where his health failed, and he died there on 28 July 1849.</p><p>He was nicknamed the Hesitant King, because he hesitated between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a> and absolute rule, and the Italian Hamlet by Giosuè Carducci. His war was the first attempt by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-savoy">House of Savoy</a> to alter the settlement of the Congress of Vienna, and his son carried it to success in 1861.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Charles Albert of Sardinia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From 1713 the dukes of Savoy were also kings, first of Sicily and from 1720 of Sardinia, but the mainland duchy, Piedmont and Nice remained the heart of the composite Savoyard state, each with its own law and institutions until the reforms of <b>Charles Albert</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-savoy">Duchy of Savoy · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>On 4 March 1848 <b>Charles Albert</b> granted the Statuto Albertino, a constitution with an elected chamber, and under Victor Emmanuel II and his prime minister Camillo Benso, Count of Cavour, from 1852, the kingdom became a working parliamentary monarchy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sardinia">Kingdom of Sardinia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Charles Albert of Sardinia</b> declared war on Austria but was defeated at Custoza on 24 July and at Novara on 23 March 1849.</blockquote><a href="/movement/unification-of-italy">Unification of Italy · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>Officially it marked the 101st anniversary of the Portoria quarter's revolt that drove the Austrians from the city; in fact it was a protest against foreign occupation and a call on <b>Charles Albert of Sardinia</b> to take up the Italian cause.</blockquote><a href="/songs/canto-degli-italiani">Il Canto degli italiani</a></li><li><blockquote>The provisional government governed Lombardy and called on <b>Charles Albert</b> of the Kingdom of Sardinia, who declared war on Austria.</blockquote><a href="/state/provisional-government-of-milan">Provisional Government of Milan</a></li><li><blockquote>The Sicilian parliament declared the Bourbon dynasty deposed in April 1848 and in July offered the crown to Ferdinando, Duke of Genoa, son of <b>Charles Albert</b> of the Kingdom of Sardinia, who declined.</blockquote><a href="/state/sicilian-kingdom-of-1848">Sicilian Kingdom of 1848</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porto">Porto</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/portugal">Portugal</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.08,7.68&amp;year=1831" aria-label="Turin on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4733 572 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Charles Albert of Sardinia"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5183 842h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5183 842h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.08,7.68&amp;year=1831">What was here in 1831? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
